--- text
Scheduled maintenance for [PRODUCT_NAME]

[PRODUCT_NAME] will be offline for planned maintenance from
[MAINTENANCE_START] to [MAINTENANCE_END]. Both times are given with
their timezone. If that is not your own timezone, convert them
before you plan around the window.

Maintenance window

Starts: [MAINTENANCE_START]
Ends: [MAINTENANCE_END]
Duration: [MAINTENANCE_DURATION]
Affected: [AFFECTED_SCOPE]

The window above is the maximum, not the plan. The work often
finishes sooner, and [PRODUCT_NAME] comes back as soon as it is
done. Nothing waits for [MAINTENANCE_END] once the work is over.

What stops during the window

- Sign-in and the web app return an error.
- The API returns an error on every request.
- Scheduled jobs and webhooks are held, then run after the window
closes.

What keeps working

- Your data stays where it is. Nothing is deleted or changed.
- Work you saved before [MAINTENANCE_START] is there when the
window closes.
- The status page stays up for the whole window.

Before [MAINTENANCE_START], finish any long-running job or postpone
it until after [MAINTENANCE_END]. An export, an import or a bulk
edit that is still going when the window opens will fail part way
through.

If your own software calls the API, expect errors for the length of
the window and let it retry afterwards, rather than treating the
failure as final.

Open the status page:

[STATUS_PAGE_URL]

The status page is where the work is reported while it runs, and
where the end of the window is confirmed. This is the only email we
plan to send about it.

Questions before the window opens? Reply to this email, or write to
[SUPPORT_EMAIL]. A real person reads every message.

[SIGNOFF_NAME], [COMPANY_NAME]
